relevance_sections = [
 ('decision','The decision',section('decision','01','Start with the job. Then choose the technology.', '''<p>A legal professional needs the correct, authorized, up-to-date source—even when they remember its meaning instead of its title. Semantic retrieval can connect paraphrases. It cannot find an absent email, repair permissions, or establish which contract is signed.</p><div class="decision-box"><span class="micro-label">My proposed decision</span><p>Improve lexical search first. Add hybrid retrieval selectively where meaning mismatch is the demonstrated bottleneck.</p></div><div class="two-col"><div class="small-panel"><div class="micro-label">Known document</div><h3>“Acme NDA 2025”</h3><p>Prioritize exact filenames, matter IDs, and citations. A thematically similar agreement is the wrong result.</p></div><div class="small-panel"><div class="micro-label">Remembered meaning</div><h3>“Termination without a penalty”</h3><p>Test semantic candidates against a clause that says “may withdraw without an early exit fee.” Retrieve the original passage.</p></div></div>''')),
 ('workflow','Search workflow',section('workflow','02','Diagnose the miss before adding AI.', '''<p>The same complaint—“I cannot find my document”—can point to entirely different failures. This conceptual workflow separates content correctness from retrieval intelligence.</p><div class="workflow"><span class="micro-label">Conceptual workflow / Fictional task</span><div class="workflow-query">What is the user trying to find?</div><div class="flow-node"><strong>Classify the information need</strong><small>Exact item · Meaning · Context · Metadata</small></div><div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div><div class="flow-node"><strong>Is the source indexed, current, and authorized?</strong><small>Coverage, freshness, and access come first</small></div><div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div><div class="flow-branches"><div class="flow-node no-ai"><strong>No → Repair the foundation</strong><small>Fix coverage, metadata, or permissions</small></div><div class="flow-node"><strong>Yes → Test lexical retrieval</strong><small>Evaluate the labeled query and rank</small></div></div><div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div><div class="flow-node yes-ai"><strong>If meaning mismatch persists, test hybrid retrieval</strong><small>Keep the lexical path when it is sufficient</small></div><div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div><div class="flow-node"><strong>Evaluate → inspect the source → verify the task</strong><small>If a gate fails, diagnose, revise, and retest</small></div><p class="flow-gate">Exact matches · Permission checks · Latency · Cost<br>Any confirmed unauthorized exposure blocks release.</p></div>''')),
 ('evaluation','Quality standard',section('evaluation','03','Define “good” before the experiment.', '''<p>The north-star outcome would be <strong>Verified Search Task Success</strong>: can the user correctly identify and open the requested authorized source with the required matter and version evidence? Click-through rate alone cannot answer that question.</p><div class="two-col"><div class="small-panel"><div class="micro-label">Assumed benchmark</div><h3>3,000 fictional records · 240 queries</h3><p>60 known-item, 70 semantic, 45 contextual, 35 metadata, and 30 difficult queries. This is an evaluation design, not a production traffic mix.</p></div><div class="small-panel"><div class="micro-label">Evaluation discipline</div><h3>Adjudicated labels. A protected holdout.</h3><p>Two hypothetical reviewers judge relevance from 0–3. Tune on a development set; deduplicate close paraphrases across splits and report uncertainty.</p></div></div><h3>Assumed release gates</h3>''' + table(['Dimension','Illustrative gate'],[
 ['Semantic relevance','≥10% relative NDCG@10 improvement'],['Known-item accuracy','Success@1 decline ≤1 percentage point'],['Latency','p95 ≤650 ms'],['Cost','≤2× baseline cost/query'],['Authorization','Zero failed critical ACL tests']
 ]) + '<p class="fine-print">Actual gates require baseline measurement, user tolerance testing, and cost analysis. Offline synthetic labels cannot establish real-user task success.</p>')),
 ('experiment','Experiment explorer',section('experiment','04','The attractive result I would not ship.', experiment('relevance','A better headline. A worse first iteration.', [('ndcg','Relevance'),('recall','Recall'),('exact','Exact matches'),('metadata','Metadata'),('latency','Latency'),('cost','Cost')],relevance_rows,[(58,'0.58'),(71,'0.71'),(69,'0.69')]) + '''<div class="decision-box"><span class="micro-label">Decision on the simulated v1</span><p>Do not ship. A roughly 22% semantic relevance gain does not compensate for a seven-point exact-item regression, or for exceeding latency and cost budgets.</p></div><p>In the imagined error review, 12 of 60 known-item queries lose their correct first result. That is a query-level failure pattern, distinct from the aggregate Success@1 change. I would test three hypotheses: vector scores overpower identifiers, short queries take the wrong route, and reranking adds unnecessary expense.</p>''')),
 ('iteration','The iteration',section('iteration','05','Improve by narrowing where AI is used.', '''<ol class="decision-list"><li><div><strong>Route identifiers to lexical search.</strong><p>Use deterministic detection for high-confidence matter IDs, filenames, and citations. Cap semantic influence when an exact match exists.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Preserve structured constraints.</strong><p>Signed status, dates, and authorization must remain hard filters. An unknown status stays unknown.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Spend reranking where it adds value.</strong><p>Use it for complex exploratory queries only when an ablation supports the added latency and cost. Keep caches within the correct authorization context.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Help people verify the source.</strong><p>Display grounded matched passages, canonical links, matter context, and authoritative version metadata. No generative answer cards in the first release.</p></div></li></ol><p>The simulated v2 gives up some semantic gain to restore exact precision and meet the assumed operational gates. That is a trade-off worth investigating, not a claim of success.</p><div class="decision-box"><span class="micro-label">Conditional recommendation</span><p>Investigate a narrow rollout only after a new holdout, access-control checks, shadow testing, and real-task validation.</p></div>''')),
 ('execution','Execution & ownership',section('execution','06','A roadmap with decisions built in.', '''<p>Assumed setting: one PM, one designer, five search engineers, two ML engineers, and security support over 12 weeks. This is a planning exercise, not a company staffing claim.</p><div class="timeline"><div class="timeline-stage"><div class="micro-label">Weeks 1–3</div><h3>Map failures and approve the evaluation.</h3><p>Define synthetic labels, instrument traces, and test the actual source of missed searches.</p><span class="timeline-gate">Gate: is meaning mismatch a material, addressable failure?</span></div><div class="timeline-stage"><div class="micro-label">Weeks 4–6</div><h3>Compare retrieval options and ablations.</h3><p>Evaluate lexical improvements and hybrid v1 by query segment.</p><span class="timeline-gate">Gate: segment uplift without critical regression?</span></div><div class="timeline-stage"><div class="micro-label">Weeks 7–9</div><h3>Iterate routing, ranking, and verification UX.</h3><p>Retest on a protected holdout and review worst-query failures weekly.</p><span class="timeline-gate">Gate: quality and operational thresholds passed?</span></div><div class="timeline-stage"><div class="micro-label">Weeks 10–12</div><h3>Shadow test, observe tasks, decide.</h3><p>Use a limited randomized rollout only with safe exposure, sufficient power, and prespecified rollback conditions.</p><span class="timeline-gate">Gate: evidence to expand, or a reason to roll back?</span></div></div><h3>What I would own</h3><p>User-value definition, prioritization, acceptance thresholds, and documented trade-offs. Search and ML engineers own implementation quality; design tests verification usability; security and privacy validate data handling. Launch decisions are joint and evidence-gated.</p><p class="case-footer-note">Still unresolved: real-user impact, uncertainty in the small benchmark, critical-citation regressions, deleted-file behavior, and sufficient statistical power to rule out known-item harm.</p>''' + source_links('beyond-keywords'))),
]

